# Run transcript: a-http-depth walkthrough

- **Date:** 2026-10-09
- **Environment:** clean — stock Python 3.12.3, curl 8.5.0, OpenSSL (system), loopback only (127.0.0.1). No Docker, no browser, no external network touched.
- **Target:** `server.py` — a ~60-line Python stdlib HTTP server written for this lesson (routes: `/`, `/login`, `/echo`, `/old`, `/new`, `/private`, `/admin`; PUT/DELETE/PATCH return 405). Served on 127.0.0.1:8321 (plain) and 127.0.0.1:8443 (TLS-wrapped with a throwaway self-signed cert).
- **Result:** all 10 walkthrough steps ran. Nothing marked "not run by us."

Note: the stdlib server speaks `HTTP/1.0` in its status lines (visible below) — the semantics exercised (methods, status codes, headers, cookies, JWT) are version-independent per RFC 9110.

---

## Step 1 — server up

`python3 server.py &` → `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" http://127.0.0.1:8321/` → `200`

## Step 2 — raw request/response

```
$ curl -s -i http://127.0.0.1:8321/
HTTP/1.0 200 OK
Server: LabServer/1.0 Python/3.12.3
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:40:37 GMT
Content-Type: text/plain
Content-Length: 6

hello
```
Status line, headers, blank line, body — each identified. `Server` header volunteers implementation info (reconnaissance note).

## Step 3 — 301 redirect

```
$ curl -s -i http://127.0.0.1:8321/old
HTTP/1.0 301 Moved Permanently
...
Location: /new
```
`curl` without `-L` does not follow; `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8321/new` → `you followed the redirect`. Redirect-following is client behavior.

## Step 4 — status code map

```
missing page: 404
admin page: 403
```
`/private` → `401` with `WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="lab"`. 401/403/404 distinguished as specified.

## Step 5 — 405 + Allow header

```
$ curl -s -i -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:8321/
HTTP/1.0 405 Method Not Allowed
...
Allow: GET, POST

method not allowed
```
DELETE and PATCH → same 405.

## Step 6 — login: JSON body, Set-Cookie + JWT

```
$ curl -s -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8321/login \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"user":"alice","pass":"wonderland"}'
HTTP/1.0 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Set-Cookie: session=alice-sess-abc; HttpOnly; Path=/

{"token": "eyJhbGciOiAiSFMyNTYiLCAidHlwIjogIkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiAiYWxpY2UiLCAiaWF0Ijog...<truncated>"}
```
Wrong password → `401`. Both session mechanisms (opaque cookie + JWT) observed in one response.

## Step 7 — JWT dissection (Python stdlib)

```
header : {'alg': 'HS256', 'typ': 'JWT'}
payload: {'sub': 'alice', 'iat': 1791571245, 'exp': 1791574845}
exp in future: True
signature valid: True
tampered payload accepted: False
```
Split on `.`, base64url-decoded, HMAC-SHA256 recomputed with the lab secret → match. One flipped payload character → signature mismatch → rejected. (Lab secret disclosed in-lesson for reproducibility; on a real system the secret is the whole scheme.)

## Step 8 — echo and Content-Type

`POST /echo` with `Content-Type: text/plain`, body `hello body` → body round-trips byte-identical. Sending JSON labeled `text/plain` is accepted by the echo — a real app would *parse* by the header; header/body mismatch is where parser differentials live.

## Step 9 — TLS

Throwaway self-signed cert: `openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -subj "/CN=127.0.0.1"` (1-day validity). Server wrapped with `ssl.SSLContext(PROTOCOL_TLS_SERVER)` on 127.0.0.1:8443.

```
$ echo | openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:8443 -tls1_3
subject=CN = 127.0.0.1
issuer=CN = 127.0.0.1
New, TLSv1.3, Cipher is TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384

$ curl -sk https://127.0.0.1:8443/
served over TLS
http_code=200
```
`-k` skipped certificate verification (self-signed); without `-k` curl refuses. On the real internet `-k` volunteers for interception.

## Step 10 — layering proof

```
$ curl --max-time 5 http://127.0.0.1:8443/
plain http to tls port: 000 (handshake failed)
```
Plain HTTP against the TLS port fails silently — the handshake precedes any HTTP byte; there is no plaintext fallback.

## Cleanup

All three server processes stopped (`kill` on recorded PIDs); ports 8321/8443 verified free afterwards. No external connections were made at any point.
